      "writtenOn": "2026-09-29T22:29:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please check the pool index too, the same way vlib_add_trace_inline() does it:\n\n      u32 trace_index \u003d vlib_buffer_get_trace_index (b);\n\n      if (vlib_buffer_get_trace_thread (b) \u003d\u003d vm-\u003ethread_index \u0026\u0026\n          !pool_is_free_index (tm-\u003etrace_buffer_pool, trace_index))\n        {\n          vlib_trace_header_t **h \u003d\n            pool_elt_at_index (tm-\u003etrace_buffer_pool, trace_index);\n\n          dtt-\u003epcap_buffer \u003d format (dtt-\u003epcap_buffer, \"%U%c\",\n                                     format_vlib_trace, vm, h[0], 0);\n          string_count++;\n        }\n\npool_is_free_index() returns 1 for a NULL pool, so it covers the NULL\ncase. It also covers a stale index after \"clear trace\": that command\nfrees the pool, and when the pool grows again with fewer elements, the\nold index points past its end. pool_elt_at_index() only ASSERTs in debug\nbuilds, so a release build reads out of bounds. With this check,\n\"if (h[0] !\u003d NULL)\" (line 159) is not necessary, because\nformat_vlib_trace() already handles an empty trace.\n\nThis can\u0027t detect a slot that was freed and then reused for another\npacket, e.g. in post-mortem mode, which drops all traces every main\nloop. Then the pcap record shows the other packet\u0027s trace. But that is\nthe same trade-off vlib_add_trace_inline() makes, and it is memory-safe.",
      "revId": "26a3a7a017762dbe28bd09b7732305018ba75b8d",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
